### TempoCNN model  (downloaded, not part of this repository)
- Upstream: Essentia `deeptemp-k16-3` (TempoCNN, MTG-UPF), https://essentia.upf.edu/models.html
- License: Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/) — NOT AGPL-3.0
- The model is an independent work aggregated with the program's output rather than a derivative of
  the AGPL code, and it keeps its own license. Upstream states that all models created by the MTG
  are licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and are available under a proprietary license on request.
- Namioto ships no model at all: `namioto-cli tempocnn`, and the editor's TempoCNN tempo algorithm,
  download `tempocnn-onnx.zip` from this project's `models` release into the data directory
  (`platformdirs.user_data_dir("namioto")/models/tempocnn`, so
  ~/.local/share/namioto/models/tempocnn on Linux). That zip carries this license text, and
  `LICENSE-CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0.txt` in this repository is a copy of it.
- Consequence: running the TempoCNN algorithm produces non-commercial output, and the `models`
  release is a non-commercial distribution. The program itself - the AGPL-3.0 sources, the wheel and
  the frozen builds - contains none of the models named here.

### GAME (Generative Adaptive MIDI Extractor)
- Upstream: Team OpenVPI, https://github.com/openvpi/GAME
- License: MIT (code) / Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/) — the models are NOT AGPL-3.0
- Used for: extracting notes from a singing voice. `namioto/game.py` is a port of the standalone
  `infer_onnx.py` that goes with GAME's ONNX model exports - GAME itself is a PyTorch project and
  ships no ONNX inference - so the module keeps GAME's MIT license, while the models stay a separate
  work under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. The models are not redistributed here: `namioto-cli game` downloads the
  size that was asked for (small 46 MB / medium 180 MB / large 362 MB) from GAME's GitHub release
  into the data directory - `platformdirs.user_data_dir("namioto")/models/game/<size>`, so
  ~/.local/share/namioto/models/game/small on Linux - and a directory of them can also be given
  directly with `--model` or `$NAMIOTO_GAME_MODEL`.
- Consequence: a transcription produced with those models is non-commercial, exactly as with the
  downloaded TempoCNN model above.

### FA-Kara (forced alignment)
- Upstream: moriwx/FA-Kara, https://github.com/moriwx/FA-Kara
- License: MIT
- Used for: the alignment core `namioto/align.py` ports. `align()`'s per-token flow - flatten a
  line's tokens into characters, force them onto the model's frames, group the frames back into one
  span per token - is reimplemented here in numpy and ONNX Runtime, with the audio windowed by the
  caller. The module loads a converted ONNX graph from the data directory
  (`~/.local/share/namioto/models/<model>/<language>`, via `platformdirs`), from
  `$NAMIOTO_ALIGN_MODEL`, or, when neither has one, from this project's `models` release
  (`mms-onnx-int8.zip`, `yohane-onnx-int8.zip`, and a half-precision `mms-onnx-fp16.zip` or
  `yohane-onnx-fp16.zip` that a GPU run loads in its place), which it downloads.
  `scripts/export_align_model.py` produces those graphs from the two models below, and
  `scripts/upload_models.sh` publishes them.

### torchaudio (CTC forced alignment)
- Upstream: pytorch/audio, https://github.com/pytorch/audio
- License: BSD 2-Clause
- Used for: `forced_align` and `merge_tokens`, whose numpy port in `namioto/align.py` computes the
  CTC trellis and the token spans. torchaudio is not a dependency of the program; only
  `scripts/export_align_model.py` uses it, to convert the MMS checkpoint.

### MMS forced-alignment model (redistributed, converted)
- Upstream: Meta AI, `torchaudio.pipelines.MMS_FA` (the `ctc_alignment_mling_uroman` checkpoint)
- License: Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) — NOT AGPL-3.0
- Namioto's default aligner (`--model mms`). `scripts/export_align_model.py` converts it from the
  network, and this project publishes the converted graph as `mms-onnx-int8.zip` on its `models`
  release;
  the original checkpoint is never shipped. Conversion changes nothing about the license, so the
  published graph stays non-commercial, with the attribution above.

### yohane karaoke alignment model (redistributed, converted)
- Upstream: `NextFire/mms-300m-ForcedAligner-karaoke-ja-Latn`,
  https://huggingface.co/NextFire/mms-300m-ForcedAligner-karaoke-ja-Latn
- License: Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/) — NOT AGPL-3.0
- Namioto's `--model yohane`, converted from HuggingFace by `scripts/export_align_model.py` and
  published as `yohane-onnx-int8.zip` on this project's `models` release, under the same terms as MMS.
- Consequence: an alignment produced with a converted aligner is non-commercial, like TempoCNN's and
  GAME's models.

